#	/etc/crontab — mon guest
#
#	Read by cron(8) directly, so it carries a "user" column.  This file
#	REPLACES the one the installer wrote: diff it against the stock file on
#	your release before deploying.
#
#	minute hour mday month wday user command

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ verify the checks
# A monitoring host whose own data collection has stopped is worse than no
# monitoring: the graphs go flat and everyone assumes the lab is quiet.  This
# compares the newest collectd RRD with "now" and complains when it is stale.
#
# The three helper scripts below are site-specific and are NOT shipped in this
# repository (see the notes at the bottom), so the lines stay COMMENTED rather
# than failing every night.
#*/15	*	*	*	*	root	/usr/local/sbin/mon-selftest

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ retention
# collectd writes one RRD per metric; the CSV tree grows without limit.  Keep
# 30 days of CSV locally (the aggregates are in the RRDs, and the storage guest
# has the long-term copy).
0	3	*	*	*	root	/usr/bin/find /var/db/collectd/csv -type f -mtime +30 \
	-delete

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ daily digest
# A short "is anything on fire" page: interface errors, hosts that stopped
# answering, disks above 85%.  Mail goes through the gateway's smtpd(8).
#0	7	*	*	*	root	/usr/local/sbin/mon-digest

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ weekly poll check
# Prove that the credential the poller uses still works, rather than assuming
# it will when a real incident needs it.
#0	8	*	*	1	root	/usr/local/sbin/mon-pollcheck

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ notes
# * The three helper scripts (mon-selftest, mon-digest, mon-pollcheck) are
#   small and site-specific; write them on the guest and keep them out of this
#   repository.  Each should exit non-zero (so cron mails root) rather than
#   printing a warning nobody reads.
# * If you add node_exporter or a dashboard, run it as its own service with its
#   own uid and add a matching pf rule in vms/mon/etc/pf.conf — this guest is
#   already the most trusted one in the lab.
